    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d148">

                <pb facs="0327" n="201" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg201"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">463.00 R 29/181</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Unofficial Representative on the Reparation
                        Commission</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hill</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-12-07"><hi rendition="#italic">7 December,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received December 16.]</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Supplementing my letter to the Department
                    of October 30th last concerning the question of Liberation Bonds, (share of the
                    cessionary States of the former Austro-Hungarian Monarchy), I have the honor to
                    enclose for the Department’s information, two copies of a communication! dated
                    December 5, 1925,<anchor n="17" xml:id="d148-f1"/> transmitting a letter from
                    the British Delegation to the Reparation Commission, dated December 3,
                        1925,<anchor n="17" xml:id="d148-f2"/> in which it is stated that the
                    British Government takes the view that it would be preferable not to call for
                    the issue of the bonds in the present circumstances (F. S. Document 60–a).</p>
                <p>A copy of these documents has been transmitted to the Embassy here for its
                    information.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ralph W. S. Hill</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
